What are the key features and clinical significance of Hemoglobin E Disorders, including their interaction with β-thalassemia?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","Hemoglobin E (HbE) disorders are a genetic trait characterized by a mutation in the beta-globin group of hemoglobin. They are characterized by the inheritance of a single copy of the HbE gene, which results in the production of a defective beta-globin protein. Hemoglobin E is a recessive trait, meaning that two copies of the HbE gene must be inherited for the disease to occur.

Hemoglobin E disorders include three main genotypes:

1. Sickocyte Ethalin (HbE-HbE)
2. Heminoozygous (HbE-HbHb)
