","Salinolol (Sildenafil) is a non-competitive antagonist of the P2X1 receptor. This means that it binds to the receptor at a specific site, but not the binding site for the ligand (e.g., ATP), and prevents the receptor from binding to the ligand. This binding reduces the receptor's ability to activate the intracellular signaling pathways that are activated by the ligand. This can lead to a decrease in the activity of the receptor and the subsequent downregulation of the signaling pathways that are activated by the receptor. This can have a variety of pharmacological effects, depending on the receptor's role in the brain and other tissues. For example, Sildenafil can be used to treat pain, inflammation, and inflammation-induced inflammation. It can also be used to treat the development of neuro-inflammatory diseases such as Alzheimer's disease and Alzheimer's syndrome.",ground_truth_similarity,"# Task Description
