     - \(\theta = \text{observed rotation in degrees}\)
     - \(n = \text{refraction index of the solution}\)
     - \(l = \text{length of the polarimeter tube in millimeters}\)
     - \(\text{specific density} = \text{density of the solution divided by the density of the solvent}\)
   - If the optical rotation of the product is the same sign and similar magnitude to the starting material, the configuration is retained. If the sign is opposite, the configuration is inverted.

2. ** Chiral Chromatography (HPLC or HPLC):**
   - The most common and reliable method is using chiral chromatography.
   - **Chiral HPLC (High-Performance Liquid Chromatography)**: This involves injecting the mixture containing the racemic mixture (if the starting material is achiral) or the racemic mixture of the product (if the starting material is chiral) into a chiral column. The chiral column contains a chiral stationary phase that interacts differently with the two enantiomers, leading to separation. By comparing the elution times of the two isomers, you can determine their configuration.
