δ = |3.44 - 2.40|
δ = 0.30

A difference of 0.30 is considered polar.

The difference in electronegativity between oxygen and carbon is 0.30. This difference is less than 0.5, which indicates a polar bond. Since oxygen is more electronegative, it will attract the shared electrons more strongly, resulting in a partial negative charge (δ-) on the oxygen atoms and a partial positive charge (δ+) on the carbon atom.

However, the carbon-oxygen bonds in carbon dioxide (CO2) are arranged linearly (CO2), and the molecule has a linear structure (O=C=O). This arrangement makes the bond dipoles are equal and opposite, resulting in a net charge of zero and the molecule is nonpolar.

