To calculate the standard enthalpy change for the reaction, we can use the following equation:

ΔH° = Σ ΔHf(products) - Σ ΔHf(reactants)

where ΔH° is the standard enthalpy change, and ΔHf(products) and ΔHf(reactants) are the standard enthalpies of formation of the products and reactants, respectively.

For the given reaction:

CaO(s) + H2O(l) → Ca(OH)2(s)

ΔH° = ΔHf(Ca(OH)2(s)) - [ΔHf(CaO(s)) + ΔHf(H2O(l))]

Now, we can plug in the given standard enthalpies of formation:

ΔH° = (-986.2 kJ/mol) - (-635.1 kJ/mol + (-285.8 kJ/mol))

ΔH° = -986.2 kJ/mol - (-980.9 kJ/mol)

ΔH° = -986.2 kJ/mol + 980.9 kJ/mol

