","The reliability function \( R_S(t) \) represents the probability that the system is in the state of ""operational"" at time \( t \). For non-relignedable systems, the failure rate is constant, and the lifetime of each component is given by \( R_i(t) = e^{-t/t_i} \), where \( t_i \) is the mean time-life of the component.  

(a) **Serial Series""
In a series circuit, the system fails if one or more of its components fail. For the survival of the system, all components must survive.  
\[
R_S(t) = R_1(t) \times R_2(t) \times R_3(t) \times R_4(t) \times R_5(t)
\]  
where \( R_i(t) = e^{-t/t_i} \) for each component.  
Substituting:  
\[
R_S(t) = e^{-t/t_1} \times e^{-t/t_2} \times e^{-t/t_3} \times e^{-t/t_4} \times e^{-t/t_5}
\]  
Simplifying:  
\[
R_S(t) = e^{-\left(1/t_1 + 1/t_2 + 1/t_3 + 1/t_4 + 1/t_5\right) t}
\]  
For a series circuit, the mean time-life of the system is:  
\[
t_S = \frac{1}{1/t_1 + 1/t_2 + 1/t_3 + 1/t_4 +/ t_5}
\]    
Thus:  
\[
R_S(t) = e^{-t/t_S}
\]  

(b) **Parallel Series-Parallel
In a parallel circuit, the system is fully-safe if one or more of its components survive. The system fails only if all components fail.  
\[
R_S(t) = R_1(t) \times R_2(t) \times R_3(t)
\]  
Substituting:  
\[
R_S(t) = e^{-t/t_1} \times e^{-t/t_2} \times e^{-t/t_3}
\]  
Simplifying:  
\[
R_S(t) = e^{-\left(1/t_1 + 1/t_2 + 1/t_3\right) t}
\]  
For a parallel circuit, the mean time-life of the system is:  
\[
t_S = \frac{1}{1/t_1 + 1/t_2 + 1/t_3}
\]  
Thus:  
\[
R_S(t) = e^{-t/t_S}
\]  

