To prepare a secondary or trans-substituted secondary deuterium-labeled allyne starting from an alkene, the following method can be employed:  
1. **Reduction of the alkene to an alkene**: The alkene is reduced to an alkane using a reducing agent such as hydrogen gas (H₂) with a catalyst (e.g., Pd/C) or a reducing agent like Li-lithium-aluminum hydride (LiLiAlH₄).  
2. **Reduction of the alkene to a terminal alkene**: The resulting alkne is reduced to a terminal alkene using a reducing agent such as hydrogen gas (H₂) with a catalyst (e.g., Pd/C) or a reducing agent like Li-lithium-aluminum hydride (LiLiAlH₄).  
3. **Reduction of the terminal alkene to a terminal alkyne**: The terminal alkene is reduced to a terminal alkyne using a reducing agent such as hydrogen gas (H₂) with a catalyst (e.g., Pd/C) or a reducing agent like Li-lithium-aluminum hydride (LiLiAlH₄).  
4. **Reduction of the terminal alkene to a deuterated terminal alkyne**: The tertiary alkyne is reduced to a deuterated tertiary alkyne using a reducing agent such as deuterium gas (D₂) with a catalyst (.., Pd/C) or a reducing agent like Li-lithium-aluminum hydride (LiLiAlH₄).  

Thus, the final deuterated tertiary alkyne is obtained.  

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
\text{Alkene} \rightarrow \text{Alkane} \rightarrow \text{Terminal alkene} \rightarrow \text{Terminal alkyne} \rightarrow \text{Disubstituted deuterated alkane}
\end{aligned}
}
