1. Temperature-responsive polymers: These polymers undergo changes in their properties based on temperature. The most common type is thermo-responsive polymers, which have a lower temperature at which they are in a swollen state and a higher temperature at to which they are in a shilled state. This property is due to the reversible phase transition of the polymer from a swollen, swollen state to a shull, glassy state. The temperature at which this transition occurs is known as the lower temperature (LC). The LC can be tuned by modifying the molecular weight, chemical structure, and crosslinking density of the polymer. For example, the LC of poly(N-isopropylacrylamide) (PNIPAM) can be adjusted by introducing functionallinkers or by incorporating other functional molecules into the polymer chain.

2. pH-responsive polymers: These polymers undergo changes in their properties based on pH. The properties of pH-responsive polymers are determined by the presence of ionizable groups, such as carboxylic acid or amine groups, in the polymer chain. These groups can be protonated or negatively charged depending on the pH of the surrounding environment, which can lead to changes in the polymer's charge distribution and its interactions with other molecules. For example, polymers with carboxylic acid groups will become more hydrophilic at high pH values, while polymers with amine groups will become more hydrophilic at low pH values. The pH range at which these changes occur can be tuned by modifying the chemical structure of the polymer or by incorporating other functional molecules into the polymer chain.

