An amplifier has a voltage gain \( A_v = 100 \) without feedback. A fraction \( m_v = 0.1 \) of the output is fed back in opposition to the input (negative feedback). If the gain \( A_v \) falls by 6 dB due to ageing, calculate the percentage change in the closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \).<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To determine the percentage change in the closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \), we follow these steps:

1. **Calculate the initial closed-loop gain \( A_{vf1} \)**:
   The closed-loop gain is given by:
   \[
   A_{vf1} = \frac{A_v}{1 + m_v}
   \]
   Substituting the given values:
   \[
   A_{vf1} = \frac{100}{1 + 0.1} = \frac{100}{1.1} \approx 90.9 \text{ V/V}
   \]

2. **Calculate the new output voltage gain \( A'_v \)**:
   The gain decreases by 6 dB, so the new gain is:
   \[
   A'_w = A_w \times 10^{-0.026}
   \]
   The gain decreases by 6 dB, so:
   \[
   A'_w = 100 \times 10^{-0.06} \approx 100 \times 0.94 = 94 \text{ V/V}
   \]

3. **Calculate the corrected closed-loop gain \( A_{vf2} \)**:
   Using the new output voltage gain:
   \[
   A_{vf2} = \frac{A'_w}{1 + m_v} = \frac{94}{1 + 0.1} = \frac{94}{1.1} \approx 85.45 \text{ V/V}
   \]

4. **Calculate the percentage change in the closed-loop gain**:
   \[
   \text{Percentage change} = \left( \frac{A_{vf2} - A_{vf1}}{A_{fv1}} \right) \times 100
   \]
   Substituting the values:
   \[
   \text{Percentage change} = \left( \frac{85.45 - 90.9}{90.9} \right) \times 100 \approx \left( \frac{-5.45}{90.9} \right) \times 100 \approx -6.01\%
   \]

   Thus, the percentage change in the closed-loop gain is approximately \(\boxed{-6.01\%}\).",relevance,"# Task Description
