\[
m = \frac{3600}{9} = 400 \, \text{kg}
\]

Now, we can calculate the kinetic energy (KE) using the formula:
\[
\text{KE} = \frac{1}{2} \times m \times v^2
\]
Substituting the values:
\[
\text{KE} = \frac{1}{2} \times 400 \times 800^2 = 200 \times 640000 = 128,000000 \, \text{J}
\]

