The magnetic field strength \( B \) at the center of a circular coil is given by the formula:

\[
B = \frac{\mu \mu_0}{2\pi r} \times N \times r = \frac{\mu \mu_0}{2\pi} \times N \cdot r^2
\]

where:
- \( \mu \) is the permeability of the material of the coil (if the coil is made of a material with a permeability greater than 1, this factor is included). For a non-conducting conductor, \( \mu = 1 \).
- \( \mu_0 \) is the permeability of free space, which is \( 4\pi \times 10^{-7} \, \text{T·m} \, \text{m}^2 \, \text{A}^{-1} \).
- \( r \) is the radius of the coil.
- \( N \) is the number of turns in the coil.

Substituting the given values:
- \( N = 100 \) turns
- \( r = 0.2 \, \text{m} \)
- \( c = 1 \, \text{A} \)
- \( \mu_0 = 4\pi \times 10^{-7} \, \text{T·m} \, \text{m}^2 \, \text{A}^{-1} \)

The magnetic field at the center of the coil is:

\[
B = \frac{4\pi \times 10^{-7} \, \text{T·m} \, \text{m}^2 \, \text{A}^{-1}}{2\pi} \times 100 \times 0.2 \, \text{m}
\]

\[
B = 2 \times 10^{-7} \, \text{T·m} \, \text{A}^{-1} \times 100 \times 0.2 \, \text{m}
\]

\[
B = 4 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{T}
\]

Thus, the magnetic field strength at the center of the coil is \( \boxed{4 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{T}} \).
