","The reference frame of the clock in the cockpit is the same as the stationary frame of the sun (or the Earth, for simplicity). The clock in the cageboard is in the same reference frame, so the time difference is negligible. 

The time dilation formula states that the observed time \( c = \frac{\Delta t}{\sqrt{1 - v^2/c^2}} \), where:
- \(\Delta t\) is the time interval in the moving frame (the sun's frame)
- \(c\) is the speed of light
- \(v\) is the speed of the moving object (the satellite)

Given:
- \(v = 0.6c\)
- \(\Delta t = 3 \text{ years}\) (time in the coordinate system of the sun)

Substituting the values:
\[
c = \frac{3}{\sqrt{1 - (0.6c)^2/c^2}} = \frac{3}{\sqrt{1 - 0.36}} = \frac{3}{\sqrt{0.64}} = \frac{3}{0.4} = 7.5
\]
Thus, the time observed on the event in the Earth's coordinate system is \(\boxed{7.5 \text{ years}}\). 

The clock in the cagebox is in the same reference frame, so the time difference is negligible. 

Final answer: \(\boxed{7.5 \text{ years}}\)",fluency,"# Task Description
